<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jonestown</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Peoples Temple Agricultural Project</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jonestown was a settlement in the forest of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> founded by the American cult the Peoples Temple under Jim Jones. On 18 November 1978, after members killed US Congressman Leo Ryan and others at a nearby airstrip, 918 people died there and in Georgetown, most by drinking poisoned fruit punch, in a mass murder-suicide.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jonestown, formally the Peoples Temple Agricultural Project, was a remote commune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> established by the Peoples Temple, an American religious organisation led by Jim Jones. Jones formed the Temple in Indianapolis, Indiana, in 1955, and it moved to Redwood Valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, in 1965 after criticism of his integrationist views. In the early 1970s it opened branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> and made San Francisco its headquarters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the autumn of 1973, after critical newspaper articles and the defection of eight members, Jones and a Temple attorney prepared a plan for a crackdown. In October 1973 the Temple's directors resolved to establish an agricultural mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a>. In the summer of 1977, under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> media investigations, Jones and several hundred members moved to Jonestown. Its population was slightly under 900 at its peak in 1978. About 70 per cent of the residents were African American. Life there changed after Jones arrived, with lessons and lectures turned to his talk of revolution and enemies.</p><p>Leo Ryan, a US congressman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, flew to Georgetown on 14 November 1978 with a delegation that included journalists, and reached Jonestown on 17 November. When the party left for the airstrip at Port Kaituma on 18 November with some defectors, Temple members murdered Ryan and four others, an act Jones had ordered.</p><p>At Jonestown itself 909 people died that day, all but two from apparent cyanide poisoning. Seventy or more were injected with poison, a third of the victims were minors, and armed guards had been ordered to shoot anyone who tried to flee. Jones and some members called the act revolutionary suicide, in a recording of the event and in earlier discussions. In Georgetown four Temple members died in a murder-suicide at the Temple's headquarters, making 918 deaths in all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Terms for the deaths have changed. Many reports at the time called them a mass suicide, while later sources speak of a mass murder-suicide, a massacre or mass murder. After the deaths the Guyanese government tended the deserted compound and for a few years in the early 1980s let Hmong refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> live there. Local people looted the buildings, which were mostly destroyed by a fire in the mid-1980s, and the ruins were left to be reclaimed by the jungle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jonestown” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1978 the mass deaths at the <b>Jonestown</b> commune of an American cult drew world attention.</blockquote><a href="/state/co-operative-republic-of-guyana">Co-operative Republic of Guyana</a></li></ul></section>
